---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import SeparatorDemo from "../../../components/SeparatorDemo";
import { codeTheme } from "../../../components/code-theme";
import { componentExamples } from "../../../data/snippets";

const { verticalExample } = componentExamples.separator;
---

<ComponentPage slug="separator">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Horizontal by default, as a native <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>.
  </Fragment>

  <SeparatorDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <strong>Semantic.</strong> A horizontal separator is an <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>, which
        screen readers announce as a separator between sections. A vertical one is a
        <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with <code>role="separator"</code> and
        <code>aria-orientation="vertical"</code>. Neither is focusable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Decorative.</strong> When the line only decorates, such as between links that are
        already separate items, pass <code>decorative</code>. It renders <code>role="none"</code>{" "}
        with <code>aria-hidden</code>, so screen readers skip it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vertical in a row.</strong> The vertical line stretches to the height of its flex
        row; give the row a height or let its content set one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Forced colors.</strong> The line is a border, not a background, so it stays visible
        in forced-colors mode.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="examples">
    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Vertical, between links</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      The links are already separate items, so the line only decorates:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={verticalExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <p slot="install-notes" class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    It creates <code>components/ui/separator.tsx</code>, the same path and prop names as shadcn's
    Separator, without Radix: the horizontal one is a native <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>. One default
    differs: <code>decorative</code> defaults to <code>false</code>, where shadcn's defaults to
    <code>true</code>, so a Buril separator is announced unless you pass <code>decorative</code>. It
    needs only your <code>cn()</code> helper; if your <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs from the
    current shadcn version, the CLI asks before replacing it, and answering no is safe.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Color</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    A separator that marks structure is a graphic people need to see, so it should reach 3:1 against
    the page. It uses the <code>border</code> color, the token meant for dividers. In the Buril
    theme that is ink, far above 3:1, which matches the engraved grid lines. For a quieter line,
    pass <code>className="border-input"</code>: Buril measures it at 3.72:1 in light mode and 3.35:1
    in dark mode. Lighter mixes fall below 3:1, so keep them for decorative lines.
  </p>
</ComponentPage>
